Paired A/B: does gating the NEON token cost anything on the film-grain path?

why           The mc_arm change is cost-identical by construction (`summon()` +
              a branch either way — `let Some(..) else` vs `.unwrap()`). The
              filmgrain_arm change is not: the token became a loop-invariant
              `Option<Arm64>` matched once per 32-pixel-block row instead of a
              bare `Arm64` summoned once per `fgy_inner_*` call. This measures
              that.

base          8568dbb (main, Merge pull request #465)
head          9ef5f42 (fix/perm-gate-aarch64)
host          Darwin 25.5.0 aarch64 (M-series), load avg ~2.9 at start — NOT an
              idle box; ratios are the claim, absolutes are not
build         cargo build --release --no-default-features \
                --features "bitdepth_8,bitdepth_16" --example profile_ivf
              (no -C target-cpu=native)
command       ~/tmp/permgate_fg_ab.sh <vector> 300 9
vector        test-vectors/dav1d-test-data/8-bit/film_grain/av1-1-b8-23-film_grain-50.ivf
              10 frames, 300 in-process iterations per rep, threads=1,
              Settings::default() => apply_grain = true (so the changed path
              really runs; verified by the film-grain permutation tests)
design        arms interleaved, order rotated every rep (base,head / head,base)
              so drift cannot favour one side; n=9 reps per arm

result        base  n=9  median 1.9126 ms/frame  min 1.8958  max 1.9880
              head  n=9  median 1.9390 ms/frame  min 1.8937  max 1.9763
              paired head/base per rep:
                1.0153 1.0015 1.0184 0.9920 1.0142 0.9889 1.0149 0.9821 0.9830
              median paired ratio 1.0015x (min 0.9821, max 1.0184)

verdict       NULL. The arm ranges overlap almost completely and the paired
              ratios straddle 1.0 in both directions. No measurable cost.
